---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "../config";
---

<Layout title="About">
  <section
    class="about-container"
    style={`--accent-color: ${siteConfig.accentColor}`}
  >
    <div class="about-image">
      <img src="/images/profile.jpg" alt="Melanie Song portrait" />
    </div>

    <div class="about-text">
    
      <h1 class="text-pretty text-xl sm:text-2xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-700">
        Hello Again!
      </h1>

      <p>
        I've always been someone who <strong>makes things</strong>. Growing up, that looked like sketchbooks and studio art classes. Later, it became spreadsheets and business models. Now, it's <strong>UX research and product design</strong> — the place where all of it finally fits together.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before design, I spent years in <strong>finance and business analysis</strong>, learning to think in data, systems, and trade-offs. What I kept noticing was that the <strong>human side of decisions</strong> often got lost — and that's what pulled me toward UX.
      </p>
      <p>
        I bring both sides to the table: the <strong>analytical rigor</strong> of someone who's lived in spreadsheets, and the <strong>empathy</strong> of someone who genuinely wants to understand what frustrates people and why. As a Master's student in <strong>Human-Centered Design & Engineering at the University of Washington</strong>, I care most about <strong>research-driven design</strong> — finding the insight that reframes the whole problem.
      </p>
      <p>
        When I'm not designing, I'm cooking something new, doing puzzles, drawing, or out on a run. My mini goldendoodle supervises all of it.
      </p>

      <p class="about-cta">
        Curious what this looks like in practice? Check out my{" "}
        <a href="/projects" class="about-cta-link">projects →</a> or my{" "}
        <a href="/art" class="about-cta-link">art →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
